WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.335
In the previous lesson, you learned how

2
00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.437
to create an agent using Google's ADK.

3
00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.840
And now we're going to create multiple agents,

4
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.742
a team of agents working together.

5
00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:11.978
We're going to have a root agent
and then two sub agents.

6
00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:13.847
We'll build on what we did previously

7
00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.281
where we had a say hello agent.

8
00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:17.717
We'll introduce a new agent that can say goodbye,

9
00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.387
and then we'll have another agent that

10
00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:22.322
puts them together into one multi-agent system.

11
00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.524
As usual, we'll go ahead and start

12
00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.126
with importing the libraries that we need.

13
00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:27.961
And then of course, we'll go ahead and set

14
00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:29.229
up the LLM that we're going to be using.

15
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.831
Again, this is open AI. We'll define the

16
00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.867
LLM and we'll also go ahead and do this

17
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.602
quick sanity check by passing out a

18
00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:37.303
message to make sure that it's ready.

19
00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.006
Always fun to see what open

20
00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:41.674
AI might say on any given day.

21
00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:45.779
Usually with friendly messages,
it's pretty good about saying friendly things back.

22
00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:48.014
Next, you can set up the agent caller.

23
00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.917
We're going to use the same class that

24
00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:52.919
we defined before in Lesson 3 part 1.

25
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.288
Rather than defining all that again, we're just

26
00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:56.790
going to import the tool that we need.

27
00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:59.993
If you remember from part one, we have this helper
function make_agent_caller

28
00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:02.095
that's going to create the agent caller

29
00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:04.497
class with all that it needs to run.

30
00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.566
Okay, with everything ready, you can

31
00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.601
now begin to create the multi-agent system.

32
00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.204
You're going to create multiple specialized

33
00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:13.540
agents, each designed for a specific capability.

34
00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:16.543
You'll create one agent in charge of handling greetings,

35
00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.678
that's the say hello agent we had before.

36
00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.815
The new agent is going to take charge
of saying goodbye.

37
00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:25.952
To combine those, you're going to have
what's called a root agent

38
00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.054
and you'll hear terms like orchestrator or coordinator.

39
00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:30.990
parameter, top-level agent.
It all really means having an agent

40
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.526
that's going to wrap up to other agents

41
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.762
and then manage their execution.

42
00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.932
That root agent is going to receive the initial user request,

43
00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:41.801
the hello or whatever the user decides to say,

44
00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:44.771
and then decide how to process that either itself

45
00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.439
or by what's called delegating, handing

46
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.408
off to one of the sub agents.

47
00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:51.344
So the root agent, this top-level orchestrator

48
00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:54.981
is going to be delegating both hello and goodbye hopefully

49
00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:57.417
to the sub agents that specialize in those tasks.

50
00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:00.186
Okay, let's start to assemble our multi-agent system.

51
00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:01.988
Again, we're going to have the

52
00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.389
say hello agent that we had before.

53
00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.225
We're going to add a say goodbye

54
00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:06.693
agent and then we'll combine them together.

55
00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.729
First, we'll define the tools for our sub-agents.

56
00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.365
The first tool is the same say hello

57
00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.033
tool that we had from the previous lesson.

58
00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.470
This is the hello world function, takes in a person's

59
00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.340
name and creates a hello world message as a response.

60
00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:24.544
To that, we're going to include a say goodbye function.

61
00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:26.880
Now, this tool is going to be even simpler.

62
00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:30.683
simpler than the say_hello because
it doesn't accept any arguments whatsoever.

63
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.952
It has a constant reply, uh Goodbye from

64
00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:35.121
Cypher, no matter what you want to do.

65
00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:36.823
This is what you're going to get

66
00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:38.791
as a response saying goodbye to the user.

67
00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.494
With those two tools defined, we can now

68
00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.563
define some agents that will use those tools.

69
00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:46.633
Now, we're going to have a special agent for greeting and

70
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:49.169
a special agent for farewell.
But you'll see it's pretty straightforward.

71
00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.140
Now as we're defining these sub-agents,

72
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.442
I really want to emphasize the best practices here
and how important

73
00:02:56.442 --> 00:03:00.180
it is to have really good descriptions of each of these agents

74
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.582
and also instructions that you're handing to them.

75
00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:03.983
You're going to spend most of your time

76
00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:05.518
once you've set up any kind of multi-agent system,

77
00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:07.320
most your time is going to

78
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.422
be spent in actually optimizing these instructions.

79
00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:11.558
This is back to classic prompt engineering.

80
00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:13.560
and also this description

81
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.695
to repeat what I had said in the previous lesson,

82
00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:18.431
the description is what allows other agents to know

83
00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:20.500
what does this agent do and when

84
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.902
should I use this agent. That's called delegation.

85
00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.438
And the instructions are for the agent itself

86
00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:28.975
understand what is its purpose,
what is it trying to do,

87
00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:31.477
and what tools does it have available
and when to use them.

88
00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:33.880
So this is our greeting sub agent.

89
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.615
It's the say hello agent that has

90
00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:37.383
access to only the say hello tool.

91
00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:40.587
And then we'll add our farewell agent.

92
00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:45.058
The farewell agent is pretty straightforward.
It's similar to the hello agent.

93
00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:48.261
You can look through the description
here of what the instructions are.

94
00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:51.965
And these instructions are actually useful to take a closer look at.

95
00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:55.368
It seems obvious. Okay, your job as

96
00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:56.970
the farewell agent is to respond

97
00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.772
whenever a user says goodbye in some kind of way.

98
00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.008
Well, people say goodbye in lots of different ways.

99
00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:03.776
So this is a very small example

100
00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:05.111
of how to do some few-shot learning

101
00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:07.080
within the agent instructions.

102
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.083
So here we have in parentheses some examples like

103
00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.952
when the user uses words like bye, goodbye,

104
00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:17.090
thanks bye, or see you.
Those are all ways of saying goodbye.

105
00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:21.194
Now, most LLMs don't need this extra bit of examples,

106
00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.696
but this is a good practice to have overall to think

107
00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:26.065
how can help the LLM understand

108
00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.800
what this agent's purpose is and actually

109
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.535
when to execute and what to do

110
00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.104
in its role.

111
00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:32.872
And of course we're going to hand

112
00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:34.374
it the say_goodbye tool that we defined earlier.

113
00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:38.111
Great. You now have two sub-agents ready to go,

114
00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:40.179
and now we need to combine them. We're

115
00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.181
going to do that by defining the root agent,

116
00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.183
which is going to understand both of these sub

117
00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:46.185
agents and when to delegate a task to them.

118
00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.521
It's similar to doing tool calling,

119
00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:51.424
but the agent knows that it's talking to another agent.

120
00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.027
So the entire conversational history gets passed along.

121
00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.195
I'm going to actually control how the conversation

122
00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.997
gets passed along to the subagent.

123
00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.333
So it's a little bit different than tool call.

124
00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:03.403
But the same idea is current workflow is going to go

125
00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:05.338
from the agent who is in charge, here

126
00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.507
the root agent, to one of the sub agents.

127
00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:10.410
So this is similar to tool calling, but let's

128
00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.912
take a careful look at how agent delegation works here.

129
00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:15.615
We know that the agents themselves have described what

130
00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:17.884
their role is, like the the say hello agent

131
00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:20.620
versus the say goodbye agent, both have some understanding about

132
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.356
what it is they're supposed to do, and their descriptions.

133
00:05:23.423 --> 00:05:25.925
describe to other agents what it is their job is.

134
00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:27.860
We're going to double down on that in

135
00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.562
the instructions that we give to the

136
00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.531
root agent. The root agent is be told,

137
00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:34.334
your job is to coordinate a team of sub-agents,

138
00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:36.736
as you can see that in the instructions here.

139
00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:39.305
and that its primary goal is to be friendly.

140
00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:43.609
In order to be friendly, it has actually two specialized sub-agents.

141
00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:46.212
So you describe what those are so that the agent understands

142
00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:48.414
when it actually should use the sub-agents.

143
00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:51.317
This is like describing when you should use particular tools.

144
00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:53.019
The greeting_agent should

145
00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:55.421
be used for handling simple greetings like hello or hi.

146
00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:58.958
And of course, the farewell agent should be used for saying goodbye,

147
00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:02.228
whether that's bye, see you or anything else.

148
00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.630
And we're explicit here about saying when

149
00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.733
those messages are received from the user,

150
00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:09.569
go ahead and delegate to these sub agents to actually

151
00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:12.705
respond to the user rather than responding directly as the coordinator.

152
00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:15.775
So if this works well,
the coordinator shouldn't do anything other than

153
00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:19.178
answer general questions and
when you say hello or goodbye,

154
00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:22.181
it should delegate to these sub-agents
for actually executing response

155
00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:24.484
or generating a response to the user.

156
00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:29.055
This top level coordinator doesn't have
any tools to use whatsoever.

157
00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.457
It can only chat or delegate to the sub agents.

158
00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:33.393
We're also going to hand it the list

159
00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:35.361
of sub agents here in the sub_agents key.

160
00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:39.132
So the sub agents are the greeting_subagent
and the farewell_subagent.

161
00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.235
Now that we have a team of agents all put together

162
00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:44.537
into a multi-agent system,
let's go ahead and interact with it.

163
00:06:45.538 --> 00:06:46.873
This is similar to what we

164
00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:48.408
did in part 1 of Lesson 3,

165
00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:51.644
where we had an asynchronous function that managed a

166
00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:53.980
conversation. So here the conversation is going

167
00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:55.515
to be just what we did before.

168
00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:58.551
Hello, I'm ABK, and then later, rather than saying I'm excited,

169
00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:01.654
this transcript is going to say, thanks, bye.

170
00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:03.823
This is going to be the second user message.

171
00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:06.492
We've also added this true here.

172
00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:09.896
So, if you look at the call to this agent caller,

173
00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:12.465
the second argument that's passed in is

174
00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:14.567
whether or not to be verbose in responses.

175
00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:16.803
We're going to turn on verbosity here so that you

176
00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:18.604
can see all of what's going on behind the scenes

177
00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:21.207
so you can understand both the user message

178
00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:23.643
going in, see the delegation, see the

179
00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:25.445
tool calls and also see the responses.

180
00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:27.180
So a lot to walk through, but

181
00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.481
it's worth walking through at least once

182
00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:30.683
to get familiar with what you can

183
00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:32.585
see when you're debugging an agent interaction.

184
00:07:35.455 --> 00:07:37.223
All right, this starts off innocently enough.

185
00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.026
We get the user's message, hello I'm ABK.

186
00:07:41.427 --> 00:07:42.962
And you can see that the initial

187
00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:44.464
event comes from the friendly agent team.

188
00:07:44.464 --> 00:07:46.999
So this is the top level coordinator, so the

189
00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:49.535
team coordinator that's going to call the sub agents.

190
00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:51.737
And you can see the first action that it takes

191
00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:54.207
is that it wants to transfer_to_agent.

192
00:07:54.207 --> 00:07:56.943
So transfer or delegating to a subagent.

193
00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:59.378
And it's going to transfer to the greeting_subagent.

194
00:07:59.378 --> 00:08:02.448
So that's perfect. In response to hello, I'm ABK.

195
00:08:02.448 --> 00:08:05.785
The top level coordinator realized, I've got an agent who's really

196
00:08:05.785 --> 00:08:09.422
good at saying hello, let me transfer control over to that agent.

197
00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:11.791
So the transfer response

198
00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:14.260
doesn't have any, you know, value whatsoever, it's just a None.

199
00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:15.695
But what we should see next

200
00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:17.363
is that the greeting subagent takes control.

201
00:08:17.363 --> 00:08:20.266
So here's another event, but now from the

202
00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:21.901
from the greeting sub agent, and

203
00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:23.836
it's going to look at the transcript

204
00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.005
and see the user's message and realize that, okay,

205
00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:28.674
it's now my job to actually do something about this.

206
00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:30.543
And the greeting sub agent is going

207
00:08:30.543 --> 00:08:32.211
to respond by making a tool call.

208
00:08:32.812 --> 00:08:34.881
You can see this FunctionCall here

209
00:08:35.248 --> 00:08:36.482
and the name of the function that

210
00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:37.750
it wants to call is called say_hello,

211
00:08:38.184 --> 00:08:40.953
and it's going to pass in some arguments where the one argument

212
00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:43.422
that's passing in is the person_name is ABK.

213
00:08:44.357 --> 00:08:46.292
So the agent has realized that

214
00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:49.295
from the statement here, of course, Hello I'm ABK.

215
00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:52.265
is able to extract that ABK must be the person's name,

216
00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:54.400
and it's going to make a function

217
00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.935
call to the tool passing in that name.

218
00:08:57.837 --> 00:09:01.307
And then you can receive the response. Here's the function response,

219
00:09:01.941 --> 00:09:05.211
and the response is Hello to you, ABK. Perfect.

220
00:09:06.145 --> 00:09:08.581
Now, the greeting sub agent is done, it goes ahead

221
00:09:08.581 --> 00:09:11.050
and you can see that the final message is True.

222
00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.686
So that's the flag that says, I'm done processing this message.

223
00:09:13.686 --> 00:09:17.056
We can go ahead and terminate this particular event loop.

224
00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:21.193
And the final agent response instances
of being the response that came

225
00:09:21.193 --> 00:09:23.529
back from the tool, Hello to you, ABK.

226
00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.833
We send in the second query then or message from the user,

227
00:09:27.833 --> 00:09:29.969
and the user says Thanks, bye!

228
00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.639
And again, the top level agent is going to go ahead.

229
00:09:33.639 --> 00:09:36.309
Oh actually, no, it's still in the control
of the greeting subagent.

230
00:09:36.309 --> 00:09:38.344
So the greeting subagent itself is going to

231
00:09:38.344 --> 00:09:40.613
realize, okay, this is not something I should use.

232
00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.349
Please transfer this to the farewell subagent.

233
00:09:43.516 --> 00:09:45.518
So it's aware of the other agents

234
00:09:45.518 --> 00:09:47.787
that are available, transfers to the farewell subagent.

235
00:09:48.888 --> 00:09:51.624
and that is the end of that function response.

236
00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:56.429
Here you can see then a message from the farewell_subagent_v1 itself.

237
00:09:56.796 --> 00:09:58.831
It again is going to look at the transcript

238
00:09:58.831 --> 00:10:01.133
now that it's been given control of the current conversation.

239
00:10:01.567 --> 00:10:03.769
The first thing it's going to do is realize,

240
00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:06.205
okay, the user said goodbye, let me go ahead and

241
00:10:06.205 --> 00:10:09.108
call the say_goodbye tool
to figure out how to respond to them.

242
00:10:09.475 --> 00:10:11.877
And so here's the function_call to say_goodbye,

243
00:10:12.378 --> 00:10:15.114
and of course it takes no arguments, as expected.

244
00:10:16.616 --> 00:10:18.584
but the response to it, is the constant

245
00:10:18.584 --> 00:10:20.553
that we saw earlier in the tool definition.

246
00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:23.689
It's going to get a farewell that is Goodbye from Cypher!

247
00:10:24.957 --> 00:10:27.827
and that again is the final response for this event loop.

248
00:10:28.761 --> 00:10:30.429
And so that is going to be

249
00:10:30.429 --> 00:10:32.098
the final agent response,
Goodbye from Cypher!

