00:11:17.843 --> 00:11:20.813
The last important component
for all agent systems is having memory.

270
00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:25.251
Memory is just internal state happening
for a particular session

271
00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:27.553
across any agents that are involved in that session,

272
00:11:27.553 --> 00:11:29.355
and of course the user themselves.

273
00:11:30.289 --> 00:11:33.025
What is session state really?
Session state in Google ADK,

274
00:11:33.759 --> 00:11:36.462
by default is really just a dictionary that's available

275
00:11:36.462 --> 00:11:38.864
that you can update keys within that dictionary.

276
00:11:39.331 --> 00:11:41.434
And when you update values of those keys,

277
00:11:41.934 --> 00:11:43.803
Google ADK tracks those changes.

278
00:11:43.803 --> 00:11:46.405
It tracks deltas to the state, basically.

279
00:11:46.739 --> 00:11:48.541
and updates the overall session state

280
00:11:48.541 --> 00:11:50.676
to keep it consistent across all agents,

281
00:11:51.277 --> 00:11:53.479
whether they're running in parallel
or running sequentially

282
00:11:53.879 --> 00:11:56.382
because it's an asynchronous system at its heart,

283
00:11:56.649 --> 00:11:58.617
it manages all the state updates.

284
00:11:59.685 --> 00:12:01.721
Agents have a few different ways
of interacting with state.

285
00:12:02.488 --> 00:12:05.091
One way, the the way that I prefer and the

286
00:12:05.091 --> 00:12:07.960
primary method for interacting with state
is through the tool context.

287
00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:09.895
So whenever a tool is called,

288
00:12:10.429 --> 00:12:12.031
there's an extra parameter that's available

289
00:12:12.331 --> 00:12:15.201
that has the context within which that tool has been called.

290
00:12:15.835 --> 00:12:19.071
Given that tool context, the tool itself then has access

291
00:12:19.071 --> 00:12:21.340
to the current state or the current memory of the agent,

292
00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.476
and it can use that memory

293
00:12:23.476 --> 00:12:25.544
for actually making either different decisions,

294
00:12:25.544 --> 00:12:28.314
creating different output,
whatever is appropriate for the memory.

295
00:12:28.814 --> 00:12:31.350
The other way that agents can interact with state

296
00:12:31.684 --> 00:12:33.486
is by using an output key.

297
00:12:33.486 --> 00:12:35.588
So, rather than using a tool call

298
00:12:35.588 --> 00:12:37.757
for updating some memory or for accessing memory,

299
00:12:38.157 --> 00:12:42.161
you can take the actual output from an agent that final response,

300
00:12:42.228 --> 00:12:44.597
and rather than just turning that back as the response from

301
00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:46.232
the agent, you can save that

302
00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:48.100
into state by defining an output key.

303
00:12:48.734 --> 00:12:50.269
So when you see this final true,

304
00:12:51.637 --> 00:12:53.439
the message related to the final true

305
00:12:53.439 --> 00:12:55.474
here it'll be the text Goodbye from Cypher!

306
00:12:55.841 --> 00:12:58.410
could get used for updating state itself.

307
00:12:58.744 --> 00:13:01.113
That's very convenient sometimes and we'll do a little

308
00:13:01.113 --> 00:13:03.215
bit of that later on in some of these notebooks.

